title: Undo and redo
tags: [[Event log]]
kind: guide
aliases: undo redo
summary: mf log undo (Ctrl+Z) takes back a label of the newest change you made, whatever the watcher wrote since; mf log redo (Ctrl+Shift+Z) takes a label of the newest undo back.

```
mf -n music log undo           # which labels the newest change is in — nothing written
mf -n music log undo 42        # undo label 42: its changes still standing
mf -n music log undo plan 42   # what that would do — nothing written
mf -n music log redo           # which labels the newest undo is in
mf -n music log redo 57
```

In the GUI: <<key "log:undo" Ctrl+z>> and <<key "log:redo" Ctrl+Shift+z>>, from anywhere.

''Undo'' looks for the newest change ''you'' made that still stands — not the watcher's,
not a previous undo ([[The undo selection]]) — and offers the ''labels'' it was written in,
innermost first: the command that made it, the answer of a script it was part of, the
script's run… ([[Labels and contexts]]). Undoing a label reverts every change of it still
standing, onto the current state: the watcher's operations written since stay where they
are — they record what really happened on disk ([[Why undo does not step HEAD back]]).
Pressed again, it takes back the change before, and so on. The undo is itself written in
a label: the command's (`mf log undo 42`), or `undo of label 42`.

On the command line the label is named: `mf log undo` alone lists the candidates and
writes nothing — undo then never takes back more or less than meant. In the GUI, a change
written in a single label (an edit, one command) is undone at once; one written in several
(a command inside a script's answer, inside its run) asks which, in the command input's
completion, innermost first.

''Redo'' is the same over the newest undo that still stands: its labels are offered, and
redoing one reverts its undos — whatever the watcher wrote in between. Pressed again, it
takes back the undo before. A new change of yours clears what could be redone; it
re-applies undos only — not arbitrary history (that is `mf log revert`). Undo and redo
compose: undo, redo, undo lands where the first undo did ([[Why undo and redo do not oscillate]]).

`plan` with a label shows what the revert would write, and writes nothing. With nothing to
take back they say `Nothing to undo.` / `Nothing to redo.` and exit 0.

!! Two writers at once

"Newest" is the newest ''operation'', not the newest thing you did. When two clients write
at the same time — a script running while you edit by hand, two windows —, the newest
operation may be the other one's: undo then offers ''its'' labels, not the ones of what you
just did. The candidates' names say whose they are before anything is undone; name the
label you mean. In the GUI, two commands running at once — a long reconcile and a field
edited meanwhile — even share a label ([[Labels and contexts]]).

!! Files

Files come along. Undoing a trashing brings the file back from the trash, redoing it
sends it there again; undoing a move made through metafolder moves the file back
([[Trash, undo and redo]]). On the command line the move policies apply
(`--on-move-available`, default `apply`; `--on-move-unavailable`, default `ask`), with
`--metadata-only`, `--force`, `--silent` passed to the revert. Undo and redo do not ask
before moving files — `mf log revert` does —; they ask before overwriting a later write to
the same field, which `--force` skips ([[Reverting onto the current state]]). The GUI asks
nothing: a move is applied if the file is there, and applied to the metadata alone if it
is not.
